ASoC: sunxi: h713-msp: the island is lost in every standby

genpd powers pd_tvfe off in the noirq phase whatever the runtime
reference says, and the audio DSP island goes with it: on the HY310 the
audio top word 0x0614a000 read 0x700 after the boot and 0 after a
standby and the MIPS restart (09.10.2026). Without rebuild_after_sleep
the driver left its state at "running" and its bridge PCM untouched, so
the first sound through the bridge (the ALSA default) after a standby
would write AUDIF with the audio top clock down - the CPU hang of S21 -
and the stall timer of a stream that ran into the sleep reads AUDIF two
seconds after the wake (review finding Q5).

The suspend now always stops the heal worker (system_wq does not freeze)
and marks the island lost, which stops the bridge PCM, its interrupts
and its timer; without the rebuild the wake sets the state to "error",
so open, prepare and the status reads keep their hands off the dead
block, and says so. A rebuild after the standby does not come up yet
(measured: "echo 1 > reset" sets the audio top again, the DSP's mailbox
then times out), hence "until a reboot".

Built against the r2test2 kernel, W=1 without warnings; not yet measured
on the device. arbeit/review-r2-20261009/BERICHT.md.
---
--- a/sound/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-msp.c
+++ b/sound/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-msp.c
@@ -474,6 +474,7 @@
 	enum msp_state state;
 	unsigned int heal_count;
 	bool rebuild_on_resume;		/* set by suspend, taken by resume */
+	bool lost_in_sleep;		/* lock: set by suspend, taken by resume */
 	bool heal_after_sleep;		/* lock: next heal follows a wake */
 	unsigned int mbox_timeouts;
 	unsigned int mbox_reissues;
@@ -2464,11 +2465,13 @@
 /*
  * System sleep. The island hangs on pd_tvfe, and genpd powers that off in
  * the noirq phase whatever the runtime reference above says: the DSP loses
- * its patch and its graph. HDMI audio does not pass the ARM, so nothing
- * would notice; the rebuild is started here as self-healing from the patch
- * kept in RAM, and runs outside the resume path. Only with
- * rebuild_after_sleep; without it the sleep leaves the island and the
- * driver alone, and the wake says so.
+ * its patch and its graph, and the audio top word 0x0614a000 reads 0 after
+ * the wake (HY310, 09.10.2026), so an AUDIF access then hangs the CPU (S21).
+ * The island is therefore always taken as lost: the heal worker (system_wq
+ * does not freeze) is stopped before genpd cuts the power, the bridge PCM
+ * and its interrupts are stopped, and the wake either rebuilds it
+ * (rebuild_after_sleep) or leaves the state at "error", so that open,
+ * prepare and the status reads keep their hands off the dead block.
  */
 static int msp_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
@@ -2476,13 +2479,12 @@
 	bool pending;
 
 	msp->rebuild_on_resume = false;
-	if (!READ_ONCE(rebuild_after_sleep))
-		return 0;
-
 	pending = cancel_work_sync(&msp->heal);
 	mutex_lock(&msp->lock);
 	msp_pcm_island_lost(msp, true);
-	msp->rebuild_on_resume = pending || msp->state == MSP_STATE_RUNNING;
+	msp->lost_in_sleep = pending || msp->state != MSP_STATE_ERROR;
+	if (READ_ONCE(rebuild_after_sleep))
+		msp->rebuild_on_resume = pending || msp->state == MSP_STATE_RUNNING;
 	mutex_unlock(&msp->lock);
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -2499,9 +2501,11 @@
 		msp->heal_after_sleep = true;
 		schedule_work(&msp->heal);
 		dev_info(dev, "standby: the DSP is rebuilt after the wake\n");
-	} else if (msp->state == MSP_STATE_RUNNING) {
-		dev_info(dev, "standby: the DSP is not rebuilt (rebuild_after_sleep=0), its state after the wake is not checked\n");
+	} else if (msp->lost_in_sleep) {
+		msp->state = MSP_STATE_ERROR;
+		dev_info(dev, "standby: the DSP lost its power in the sleep and is not rebuilt (rebuild_after_sleep=0): state error, no HDMI audio until a reboot\n");
 	}
+	msp->lost_in_sleep = false;
 	mutex_unlock(&msp->lock);
 	return 0;
 }
